From 94eb2896ce90edbe6ea359465fde9868f858df48 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 05:02:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Add store/restaurant rating system (Zomato/Blinkit
 style), explain OTP log timing
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

1. Rating system end-to-end:
   - Backend: added rating/rating_count to StoreProfile (migration 0015).
     The food module already had Restaurant.rating_avg/rating_count
     (migration 0007) but nothing ever wrote to them — same gap on the
     grocery side once added.
   - New POST /orders/{id}/rating: customer rates a delivered order
     (1-5 stars + optional review), rolls into a running average on the
     relevant Restaurant or StoreProfile row. One rating per order —
     re-submission is rejected rather than silently overwritten, which
     would skew the average.
   - /grocery/stores and the order tracking/list responses now return
     rating + rating_count (and customer_rating on orders) so the
     frontend can decide what to show.
   - Frontend: grocery store list now shows the same RatingChip pattern
     the restaurant list already used — a green/amber star score once a
     store has at least one real rating, a neutral "NEW" chip before
     that. Never a fabricated 0.0/5.0 score, which is what would make a
     fresh store list look noisy or fake.
   - Added a small star-picker to the order detail screen for delivered,
     unrated orders ("How was your order?"), and a plain thank-you line
     once rated. The Orders list now also routes a delivered-but-unrated
     order into the detail page (pill reads "Rate" instead of "Track")
     so there's actually a place to submit it from.

2. Clarified (no code change): the delivery dashboard's repeated
   `/delivery/orders/available` log lines are the existing 10s polling
   in IncomingOrderAlert.tsx (new-order alert bell), not a bug. And the
   debug OTP log/endpoint added earlier only fires once the delivery
   partner actually starts the "Confirm Delivery" flow for a
   picked-up order — confirmed the service code is correct; nothing to
   fix there, the earlier log just hadn't reached that step yet.
